---
import KnowledgeArticle from '../../../components/knowledge/KnowledgeArticle.astro';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the difference between order to cash and quote to cash?', a: 'Quote to cash starts earlier, with configuring, pricing and quoting the deal, and typically includes contract management. Order to cash usually starts once an order is placed or a contract is signed. The boundary varies between organisations, so it is worth defining explicitly.' },
  { q: 'Who owns the order-to-cash process?', a: 'Usually no single team. Sales, operations, fulfilment, billing, credit control and treasury each own a stage. That is why hand-offs between them are where delays and leakage most often occur, and why many organisations name an end-to-end process owner.' },
  { q: 'What is days sales outstanding (DSO)?', a: 'DSO is a common measure of how long, on average, it takes to collect payment after a sale is invoiced. It is a lagging indicator: it confirms a collection problem after it has happened. Stage-level measures such as time to invoice and dispute ageing give earlier warning.' },
  { q: 'Is order-to-cash automation the same as observing it?', a: 'No. Automation speeds up individual steps. Observing the process shows how the steps perform together, where items wait and where value is lost, which tells you which steps are worth automating.' },
];
---
<KnowledgeArticle
  title="What is order to cash (O2C)?"
  metaTitle="What Is Order to Cash (O2C)? Stages and Metrics"
  description="Order to cash (O2C) explained: the stages from order to collected payment, who owns each one, the metrics that matter and where delays and leakage occur."
  crumb="Order to cash"
  dek="Every sale is a promise of cash. Order to cash is the chain of work that turns that promise into money in the bank, and it crosses more teams than almost any other process."
  definition="<strong>Order to cash (O2C)</strong> is the end-to-end business process that begins when a customer places an order and ends when payment has been received and reconciled. It typically covers order management, credit checks, fulfilment or service delivery, invoicing, collections and cash application."
  term="Order to cash"
  faqs={faqs}
  related={[
    { label: 'Revenue leakage', href: '/use-cases/revenue-leakage/', desc: 'Find where earned revenue goes missing', icon: 'revenue' },
    { label: 'Order exceptions', href: '/use-cases/order-exceptions/', desc: 'Find orders that stopped moving', icon: 'clock' },
    { label: 'Panoptes for finance', href: '/solutions/finance/', desc: 'Money movement, leakage and cost drivers', icon: 'finance' },
    { label: 'Business Graph', href: '/platform/business-graph/', desc: 'Entities, relationships and dependencies', icon: 'graph' },
    { label: 'Logistics & supply chain', href: '/solutions/industries/logistics-supply-chain/', desc: 'Orders, shipments and promised dates', icon: 'delivery' },
    { label: 'What is process mining?', href: '/resources/knowledge/what-is-process-mining/', desc: 'Reconstructing processes from event logs', icon: 'process' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>What are the stages of order to cash?</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Typical owner</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Order management</td><td>The order is captured, validated and priced</td><td>Sales operations or customer service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Credit management</td><td>The customer's credit limit and terms are checked</td><td>Credit control</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fulfilment or delivery</td><td>Goods are picked, shipped and delivered, or the service is provisioned</td><td>Operations or logistics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Invoicing</td><td>An accurate invoice is issued for what was delivered</td><td>Billing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Collections</td><td>Payment is chased, disputes are handled</td><td>Accounts receivable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cash application</td><td>Received payments are matched to invoices</td><td>Accounts receivable or treasury</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Revenue and receivables are recorded and reported</td><td>Finance</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For subscription businesses, the same chain repeats at every renewal: usage or entitlement drives an invoice, which drives a payment attempt, which may fail and enter recovery.</p>

  <h2>Why order to cash is hard to see end to end</h2>
  <p>Each stage usually lives in a different system (a CRM, an order platform, a warehouse or provisioning tool, a billing system, a payment provider, an ERP) and is measured by a different team. Each team can be meeting its own targets while the process as a whole is slowing down, because the delay sits in the hand-offs between them.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>An order is delivered but the invoice is not triggered because a status was not updated.</li>
    <li>An invoice is issued with the wrong price or purchase order reference and is disputed.</li>
    <li>A payment arrives but cannot be matched, so the customer is chased for money already paid.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Which order-to-cash metrics matter?</h2>
  <p>Outcome measures confirm how the process performed. Stage measures warn earlier.</p>
  <h3>Outcome measures</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><a href="/resources/knowledge/what-is-days-sales-outstanding/">Days sales outstanding (DSO)</a></strong>: average time from invoice to payment.</li>
    <li><strong>Bad debt and write-offs</strong>: revenue that will not be collected.</li>
    <li><strong>Order-to-cash cycle time</strong>: elapsed time from order to reconciled cash.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Stage measures</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Time from delivery to invoice</strong>, and the count of delivered-but-not-invoiced orders.</li>
    <li><strong>Invoice accuracy</strong>: the share of invoices issued without correction or credit note.</li>
    <li><strong>Dispute volume and ageing</strong>, by reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Unapplied cash</strong>: payments received but not yet matched.</li>
    <li><strong>Payment failure and recovery rates</strong> for card and direct-debit collections.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where delays and leakage occur</h2>
  <p>Delay and leakage are related but different. Delay means cash arrives later than it should. Leakage means value that was earned is never billed or collected at all: unbilled usage, missed price increases, credit notes issued in error or failed payments that are never recovered. The <a href="/use-cases/revenue-leakage/">revenue leakage use case</a> covers how to find it.</p>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Illustrative example</strong>
    A distributor's DSO rises at quarter end. Stage measures show the cause is not slow-paying customers but a growing number of invoices disputed for missing purchase order references, after a change in how orders are captured for one customer segment.
  </div>

  <h2>How to improve the order-to-cash process</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Agree where the process starts and ends, and name an end-to-end owner.</li>
    <li>Define the business event that marks entry to and exit from each stage.</li>
    <li>Measure waiting time at each hand-off, not only work time inside each team.</li>
    <li>Track items that are stuck between stages, such as delivered-not-invoiced, as a live list with owners.</li>
    <li>Change one thing at a time and check whether the stage measure moved.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How it relates to business observability</h2>
  <p>Order to cash is a natural first journey to make observable, because it connects customers, operations and money. Panoptes is designed to model it as connected entities (order, shipment, invoice, payment) in the <a href="/platform/business-graph/">Business Graph</a>, follow each order end to end with <a href="/platform/journey-intelligence/">Journey Intelligence</a> and show where items wait with <a href="/platform/process-intelligence/">Process Intelligence</a>, so that a change in fulfilment can be traced to its effect on billing and cash.</p>
</KnowledgeArticle>
